{%- comment -%}
  Kofii Flow — Marquee shell
  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  The <kf-marquee> markup contract in one place: the viewport, the two tracks,
  and the pause control. Rendered by the marquee section and by the logo
  cloud's scrolling layout — the structure must never exist twice, because
  kf-marquee.js reads these exact hooks and a section that drifted from them
  would lose its pause button with no visible error.

  The scrolling itself is not here and never was: `@keyframes kf-marquee` in
  kf-motion.css animates two duplicated tracks with no JavaScript at all.

  Item styling is the caller's. A marquee message, a band item and a logo look
  nothing alike, so this snippet takes the items already rendered.

  The caller must load `component-marquee.css` and `kf-marquee.js`.

  Usage:
    {%- capture items -%}...{%- endcapture -%}
    {% render 'kf-marquee-track', items: items, pause_on_hover: true %}

  Parameters:
    items          (string, required)  the item markup, already rendered
    pause_on_hover (boolean)  convenience on top of the button, never instead
    edge_fade      (boolean)  fade the band out at both ends
    class          (string)   extra classes on the viewport
    host_class     (string)   classes on <kf-marquee> itself — the announcement
                              bar puts its color scheme here, because the
                              element IS the bar rather than sitting inside one
    host_style     (string)   inline custom properties on <kf-marquee>
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- liquid
  assign viewport_class = 'kf-marquee'
  if edge_fade
    assign viewport_class = viewport_class | append: ' kf-marquee--fade'
  endif
  if class != blank
    assign viewport_class = viewport_class | append: ' ' | append: class
  endif

  assign pause_label = 'general.accessibility.pause_animation' | t
  assign play_label = 'general.accessibility.play_animation' | t
-%}

<kf-marquee
  {% if host_class != blank %}class="{{ host_class }}"{% endif %}
  {% if host_style != blank %}style="{{ host_style }}"{% endif %}
  {% if pause_on_hover %}data-kf-pause-hover{% endif %}
>
  <div class="{{ viewport_class }}" data-kf-marquee>
    {%- comment -%}
      Pass 2 is the duplicate that makes the loop seamless. It is `inert` as
      well as aria-hidden so its links do not collect a second set of tab
      stops — aria-hidden alone would hide them from a screen reader while
      leaving them focusable, which is worse than either.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- for pass in (1..2) -%}
      <div
        class="kf-marquee__track"
        data-kf-marquee-track
        {% if pass == 2 %}aria-hidden="true" inert{% endif %}
      >
        {{ items }}
      </div>
    {%- endfor -%}
  </div>

  {%- comment -%}
    Hidden until <kf-marquee> reveals it: without the element there is nothing
    to pause, and offering a dead button is worse than offering none.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  <button
    class="kf-marquee-toggle"
    type="button"
    data-kf-marquee-toggle
    data-pause-label="{{ pause_label | escape }}"
    data-play-label="{{ play_label | escape }}"
    aria-label="{{ pause_label | escape }}"
    hidden
  >
    <span class="kf-marquee-toggle__icon kf-marquee-toggle__icon--pause">
      {%- render 'kf-icon', name: 'pause', size: 'sm' -%}
    </span>
    <span class="kf-marquee-toggle__icon kf-marquee-toggle__icon--play">
      {%- render 'kf-icon', name: 'play', size: 'sm' -%}
    </span>
  </button>
</kf-marquee>
